  sections={[
    {
      h2: 'What HRV breathing is',
      paras: [
        "Heart rate variability is the small change in the time between one heartbeat and the next. The Cleveland Clinic describes it as the amount of time between your heartbeats fluctuating slightly, driven by the two halves of the autonomic nervous system. It isn't your heart rate. What counts as normal HRV for one person may not be normal for someone else.",
        "HRV breathing means breathing slowly and evenly, at about five to six breaths a minute. Lehrer and Gevirtz, reviewing HRV biofeedback in 2014, put the average resonance frequency at about 5.5 breaths per minute, varying by person around 0.1 Hz. On a pacer, that's coherent breathing: 5.5 seconds in, 5.5 out, no holds.",
        "So an HRV breathing app has two jobs: pace you at that rhythm to the second, and be straight about what a phone and a watch can measure. Pacing is the easy one.",
      ],
    },
    {
      h2: 'Where an iPhone gets your HRV',
      paras: [
        "Your iPhone can't measure HRV, so any HRV app on it shows a number taken somewhere else. With an Apple Watch, that measurement is SDNN: the standard deviation of the gaps between normal heartbeats, in milliseconds. Apple's HealthKit documentation calls it the one HRV method HealthKit uses, and says the Watch records the samples by itself.",
        "To see them, open Health on the iPhone, tap Browse, then Heart, and pick the HRV entry. On Apple Watch Series 12 and Ultra 4 there's also a Recovery HRV in the watch's Heart Rate app, which Apple describes as a possible indicator of stress and recovery. Apple samples it while you're still, so the count of readings varies with how active you are.",
        "Refresher reads those SDNN samples from Apple Health and charts the last 30 days. It doesn't compute HRV itself. I'd put the same question to any HRV app on an iPhone: where does the number come from? If the app can't say, I wouldn't trust the number.",
      ],
    },
    {
      h2: 'What Refresher measures, and what it reads',
      paras: [
        "With an Apple Watch on and the heart rate switch turned on in Settings, the app keeps the first heart rate reading of the session and the last. The difference is on the completion screen and in your history. The reading needs both ends and at least three samples. Without them the row is left out.",
        "Apple Watch computes HRV and writes a handful of samples a day into Apple Health. Refresher draws the last 30 days of those, live from HealthKit, with the days you practised marked on the same chart. The chart doesn't appear at all until there are two days of data. There's no empty state and no nag to connect a Watch.",
        "Most wrist-worn trackers usually aren't sensitive enough to detect HRV accurately. A number built on a few daily samples from a wrist sensor is one to read over weeks.",
      ],
      aside: [
        "I refused to put a before-and-after HRV delta on the completion screen. Apple Watch emits a few SDNN samples a day, not a stream, so a delta for one session can't be produced honestly, and the person who wants HRV is the person who would catch it. The screen shows the measured heart rate change and your latest HRV reading, dated, instead.",
        "HRV also never leaves the device. It isn't in the iCloud-synced store.",
      ],
    },
    {
      h2: 'What the chart leaves to you',
      paras: [
        "The chart draws two series on one axis, your Apple Health SDNN and your session days, and computes no correlation between them. It doesn't tell you what the sessions did. You can read the two lines yourself, and I'd rather you did that than trust a score I made up.",
        "It's a pacer with a chart. It paces breathing and shows you numbers that Apple Watch and Apple Health already hold. If a professional is running biofeedback with you, their equipment is the one to read.",
      ],
    },
  ]}
  howTo={{
